{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>Only with all your soul's desire.</b> What is Scripture speaking of? If of unconsecrated meat — to permit it to them without the offering of emurim, it is already stated elsewhere (verse 20): ״״כִּי יַרְחִיב ה׳ אֶת גְּבוּלְךָ וְגוֹ׳ וְאָמַרְתָּ אֹכְלָה בָשָׂר וְגוֹ׳״ — what is this speaking of? Of consecrated animals that developed a blemish, that they may be redeemed and eaten anywhere. One might say they may be redeemed for a transient blemish? Scripture says: ״רַק״ (Sifrei)." "<b>You shall slaughter and eat.</b> You have no permit for their fleece and fat except eating by way of slaughter (Deut. 12)." "<b>The unclean and the clean.</b> Since they derive from the status of consecrated animals, about which it is stated ״וְהַבָּשָׂר אֲשֶׁר יִגַּע בְּכָל טָמֵא לֹא יֵאָכֵל״ (Leviticus 7), it was necessary to permit in this case that unclean and clean eat from one plate." "<b>Like the deer and like the gazelle.</b> For no offering comes from them." "<b>Like the deer and like the gazelle.</b> To exempt them from the foreleg and the cheeks and the stomach (Sifrei)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {1 "The parenthetical (בכ' ו') = בכ\"ו, abbreviation for the book of Deuteronomy (כ\"ו = 26th book in some enumerations)."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:46:48.883833843Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}